      <article class="prose">
        <p>I&#x27;m Dan Vallentyne, a marine technologist working across research software, underwater imaging, and product design.</p>
        <p>I build tools for marine science and environmental organizations. My work can begin with collecting images underwater and continue through processing, mapping, and building an interface for exploring the results.</p>
        <h2>A background in making complex work understandable</h2>
        <p>My background is in design and software development. I&#x27;ve worked on enterprise applications, educational experiences, and tools for commercial, government, and nonprofit organizations.</p>
        <p>At Morgan Stanley, I led design and research for enterprise applications. My redesign of key MyBooking workflows reduced task completion time by 60%. I also worked on shared platform patterns, mentored designers, and facilitated product development workshops.</p>
        <p>That experience shapes how I approach research tools: learn how people work, understand the information they need, and make the next step clearer.</p>
        <h2>Working with the data, from its source</h2>
        <p>My marine work includes reef surveys in Bonaire, underwater photogrammetry for Reef Check California, and marine megafauna field data collection in Mozambique.</p>
        <p>I&#x27;ve developed custom survey equipment, automated the processing of underwater imagery, and built tools for inspecting reef maps and marine debris. I&#x27;m also the first author of a study on mercury in dried market fish and a coauthor of research on marine megafauna vulnerability to tuna fisheries.</p>
        <p>At Ocean Agentics, I lead product work and volunteer research teams developing ocean data tools. I wrote and won the organization&#x27;s first grant, securing $100,000 from Docusign for collaborative workshops.</p>
        <h2>What guides my work</h2>
        <p>I&#x27;m interested in ecological change, deep time, and how people participate in thriving ecosystems across generations. These interests connect my work in the field with the software, research, and communication projects I take on.</p>
        <h2>Background</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>PADI Divemaster with more than 1,000 dives.</li>
          <li>BA, Design and Digital Application, California State University Monterey Bay, 2003.</li>
          <li>Additional work in museum interpretation, design education, coral photography, and recording whale song.</li>
        </ul>
